@@ -664,5 +665,5 @@ Record each decision here with its reason. The README draws from this list.
 | Shared popup assembly for Hub and Game (DEC-136) | Needed by DEC-135: Game cannot reference `ColorBlockJam.Hub`. `ColorBlockJam.UI` (`Assets/ColorBlockJam/UI`, next to `Common.uss` and the runtime theme) holds popups both scenes use: the Settings popup view, presenter and UXML/USS (with a HOME button hidden unless a host shows it), and later the booster purchase popup. It references Runtime, Player and Onity.Reactive only; Hub and, later, Gameplay reference it. Scene hosts adapt it (Hub: an `IHubOverlay` adapter; Game: the pause flow). |
 | CBJ Template currency review (DEC-137) | User order of 2026-09-27 ("iyi olabilecek mimari olarak olanları bize geçir"). Compared with the template's Currency module: enum `CurrencyType` keys whose order must never change, a static `CurrencyController` with static events, the balance inside each `Currency`, saves keyed by `string.GetHashCode()` and no file protection. **Adopted:** the compact formatter lives with the currencies, as the template's `CurrencyHelper` does: `Runtime/Currencies/CurrencyText` (Runtime references ZString), shared by Hub, the `ColorBlockJam.UI` popups and the Game HUD. **Queued:** the template's `CurrencyAmount` (currency + amount for prices and later rewards, one-row Inspector drawer) as `TASK-20260927-141439926-2949c75f-currency-amount-price`, after the live owner of `BoosterDefinition.cs` and `PlayerSaveService.cs` releases them. **Rejected:** enum identity (DEC-128 string IDs survive reordering); static controller and events (Rule 7); balances on the definition (DEC-92 publishes only after commit); hash save keys (`GetHashCode` is no stable contract and can collide; the snapshot keys by ID and HMAC covers integrity, DEC-130); the remote-config default override and analytics source/sink tags (no server or analytics in scope; the store port of DEC-129 is the seam); change-delta events (subscribers derive them from reactive values); per-currency floating-coin settings (presentation belongs to views); TextMeshPro sprite-atlas generation (UI Toolkit). Task `TASK-20260926-235915696-6b7f82f4-player-data-currencies`. |
 | Hub launch request and carried starting boosters (DEC-138) | User request of 2026-09-27 (whole Hub scope; references 01, 09, 10). Home's Level button opens the pre-level popup (`Hub/PreLevel`: `LEVEL N` from `PlayerProgress.GetPlayableLevelIndex`, one selector per `BoosterCatalog` entry with `PreLevel`, in catalog order). Play makes `LevelLauncher` run exactly one `OnitySceneFlow.TransitionAsync(profile, Gameplay, request)` with an immutable `LevelLaunchRequest : IOnitySceneEnterData` (`Runtime/Levels`: playable level index and the selected `BoosterId`s in selector order); a second Play, or Play while one is in flight, does nothing. `GameAttemptLoader` consumes the request once through `OnitySceneTransitionStore` before any wait; the pure `LevelLaunchResolution` chooses request, then the Level Editor's Test Level (DEC-127), then the installer's serialized index, and clamps an out-of-range request with one warning. This retires the Hub half of DEC-116 (direct Play of Game keeps the serialized index); the `NoPauseOverlay` foreground-resume half stays until the pause popup. Starting boosters are carried, not applied or spent: opening, toggling, closing and Play never debit stock, and the Game scene ignores the selection until the booster opening task prepares and commits it on Home (AC-5.7, DEC-92). Selectors are rounded squares (`Btn_settings_normal` blue with a red count badge, tinted green with a check when selected; user feedback 2026-09-27). A zero-stock selector opens the shared purchase popup (`ColorBlockJam.UI/Purchase`, DEC-136) through the Hub adapter `BoosterPurchaseOverlay`, which covers (hides) the pre-level popup until it closes so the two never overlap (reference 8); Buy calls `IBoosterShop.PurchaseAsync`, is disabled while `IsSaving`, keeps the popup open and never auto-selects (DEC-85). The pre-level presenter and the purchase adapter bind to themselves only, so `IHubOverlay` keeps one container binding; `HubScreen` registers each overlay with `HubNavigator` under its own id. `BoosterCatalog.asset` lists Extra Time first (user approval "Katalogda Extra Time'ı başa al"), so the pre-level order is Extra Time, Rocket and the in-game order stays Time Freeze, Rocket, Hammer, Vacuum. See [Hub and Player Data design](docs/Hub-And-Player-Data-Design.md) section 9. Task `TASK-20260926-235915699-78356740-hub-pre-level-launch`. |
-| Separate wall units and gate motion (DEC-139) | User request of 2026-09-27: in the reference game the walls read as separate rounded elements; ours read as one merged frame. The matching gate must sink while a block exits and rise after it breaks up. The user also said "eğer hala gerekli olursa blenderdan edit yapma hakkın var". Existing-art composition (a 4x4 strip template of Wall, Corner_1 and BlockFill) was built and rendered first; it read as recessed trays and broke the Level Editor canvas, so the approved Blender fallback was used. `EnvironmentComposer.Compose` keeps the rule placements (one rule piece per wall quadrant) for the Level Editor canvas and barriers. `ComposePlayArea`, used only by `EnvironmentView`, closes each maximal straight run of the outer boundary (void connected to the board's ring, notches included) with `Brick_End` caps whose mouths face the run and the rule's Wall pieces between them. A one-quadrant run gets two half-length caps; corners are their own units. Interior holes keep one continuous rule frame, as in references 02 and 17 (user decision). `Brick_End` comes from `ArtSource/BoardKit/WallBrick.blend` (supplied Wall profile inside Wall's exact mesh bounds, rectangular end after reference 1). `export_wall_brick.py` exports it with the kit's FBX unit scale. `Brick_End.prefab` is Wall.prefab with only the mesh replaced (`Color Block Jam > Art > Build Wall Brick`), bound in `EnvironmentDatabase` and pooled. One `EnvironmentGate` per authored door (door slots and arrow) has one PrimeTween sequence. The board presenter sinks it before a lean or exit crosses the door, holds it while any exit through that door runs, and raises it 0.15 s after the last body is gone, inside the 0.8 s Won Finish. Exits wait for the sink inside their own time; domain exit timing, events and rewards are unchanged. Suspend pauses, Teardown stops and resets, and Restart resets every gate. Reopened the same day for two review notes the user asked to fix (D-001, D-002; user choice "All outer corners"): every outer-boundary corner is `Corner_End`, Corner_1's footprint with both arm ends closed and rounded (Blender, same `WallBrick.blend`/`.fbx`, `Corner_End.prefab` from Corner_1.prefab with only the mesh replaced), so a lowered gate beside it shows a rounded end; and each wall quadrant and door slot stands on a `FloorBase` square that reaches floor, walls and gates but stops a quarter quadrant short of open void, so gaps between units read dark instead of showing the background. Supplied FBX files and the Wall, corner and door prefabs are unchanged. See [Environment Layout](docs/Environment-Layout.md). Task `TASK-20260927-123500933-c1e67965-environment-runs-gate-motion`. |
